But she, drawing back, took a stone in her stout hand,
lying on the plain, black and rough and great,
which men of earlier time set to be a boundary of the plough-land;

“Ah, child of aegis-bearing Zeus, Atrytone,
and indeed again the dog-fly is leading mortal-ruining Ares
out of hostile war, through the turmoil;

So she spoke, and Athena rushed after, and rejoiced in spirit,
and, rushing upon her, at the breast with stout hand
she struck;

when to manly Laomedon,
having come from Zeus, we worked for hire for a year
for an agreed wage;

And tearful, away from under, the goddess fled, like a dove
which from under a hawk flew into a hollow rock,
a cleft;
